{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-110127" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/assets/e09f5a90c683876f849577cd75af3c77cc21c6f9af10c5a4ece47718aa33f738.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2,4)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(4,2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(4)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-110127" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $(4,2)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(4)=2.$\nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [0,4]$ is $-\\frac{1}{2}$, hence, ${f}^{′}(2)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-110127" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fc644e02aa9c85304749abccec144ff0b1ac05836a7caf34f8e8f3110207947d"} {"question" "q-110127" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-110127" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-110127" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-110198" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/assets/ba762e13c4015b43b99f9038ee63a247584345cd535e12b80c1f3b0d9e50e70b.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(1,0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(0,1)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-110198" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $(0,1)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(0)=1.$ \nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [0,2]$ is $-2$, hence, ${f}^{′}(1)=-2.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is \n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-110198" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fc644e02aa9c85304749abccec144ff0b1ac05836a7caf34f8e8f3110207947d"} {"question" "q-110198" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-110198" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "-\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-110198" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-342575" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/assets/ce948d3eade308a90ffa85a7c7a88a8c403a6620ffda7e6d24444d2abac0770c.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(1,1)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(1,1)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-342575" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $(1,1)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(1)=1.$ \nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [-2,4]$ is $\\frac{2}{3}$, hence, ${f}^{′}(1)=\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is \n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-342575" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fc644e02aa9c85304749abccec144ff0b1ac05836a7caf34f8e8f3110207947d"} {"question" "q-342575" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-342575" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-342561" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/assets/adcee8bddcbbd96e18d63f85f5b9ebf2edc52897cc1ddcca7e377194462f6bda.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(-1,0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ The point $B$ has coordinates $(0,-1)$ and lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ If ${f}^{′}(-1)=3,$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-342561" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $B\\,(0,-1)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(0)=-1.$\nWe're also told that ${f}^{′}(-1)=3.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-342561" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fc644e02aa9c85304749abccec144ff0b1ac05836a7caf34f8e8f3110207947d"} {"question" "q-342561" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-342561" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-110129" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/assets/265866e56e07a86dd4d6f43ce5d752d62491dea962c3dce3273b924e6e2b362b.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2,3)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(3,2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(3)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-110129" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $(3,2)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(3)=2.$ \nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [0,2.5]$ is $2$, hence, ${f}^{′}(2)=2.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is \n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(3) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-110129" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fc644e02aa9c85304749abccec144ff0b1ac05836a7caf34f8e8f3110207947d"} {"question" "q-110129" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-110129" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-110129" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-342585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and the point $P(e,1)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1).$\n\n${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-342585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(e,1).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(e) & =\\frac{1}{e}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(e,1)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(1,e)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(e)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{e})} \\\\  & =e.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-342585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fc644e02aa9c85304749abccec144ff0b1ac05836a7caf34f8e8f3110207947d"} {"question" "q-342585" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-342585" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "e"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-342579" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}+1}$ and the point $P(1,0)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0).$\n\n${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-342579" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(1,0).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(x-1)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}+1)-(x-1)\\cdot {({x}^{2}+1)}^{′}}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1\\cdot ({x}^{2}+1)-(x-1)\\cdot 2x}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{2}+2x}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1+2x-{x}^{2}}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1+2(1)-{1}^{2}}{{({1}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(1,0)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(0,1)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-342579" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fc644e02aa9c85304749abccec144ff0b1ac05836a7caf34f8e8f3110207947d"} {"question" "q-342579" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-342579" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-342580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=3\\sqrt[3]{x}$ and the point $P(8,6)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(6).$\n\n${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(6)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-342580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(8,6).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3\\sqrt[3]{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-2/3} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(8) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{8}^{2}}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(8) & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(8,6)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(6,8)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(6) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(6))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-342580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fc644e02aa9c85304749abccec144ff0b1ac05836a7caf34f8e8f3110207947d"} {"question" "q-342580" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-342580" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-15652" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{3+4x}{2-3x}$ and the point $P(1,-7)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ determine the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(-7,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-15652" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(1,-7).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3+4x}{2-3x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{17}{(2-3x)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =17\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(1,-7)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(-7,1)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(-7) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-7))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-15652" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fc644e02aa9c85304749abccec144ff0b1ac05836a7caf34f8e8f3110207947d"} {"question" "q-15652" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-15652" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-7"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{7}"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "17"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{17}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-15652" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{17}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-15651" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{x-3}}$ and the point $P(7,\\frac{3}{4})$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ determine the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(\\frac{3}{4},7).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-15651" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(7,\\frac{3}{4}).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{x-3}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{3}{4(x-3)^{3/2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(7) & =-\\frac{3}{32}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(7,\\frac{3}{4})$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(\\frac{3}{4},7)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(\\frac{3}{4}) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(\\frac{3}{4}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{3}{32})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{32}{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-15651" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fc644e02aa9c85304749abccec144ff0b1ac05836a7caf34f8e8f3110207947d"} {"question" "q-15651" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-15651" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{32}{3}"] ["math" "-\\frac{3}{32}"] ["math" "\\frac{32}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-15651" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{32}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-4962" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The following table gives the values of functions $g(x)$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({g}^{-1})^{′}(-2).$\n\n| **$x$** | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $4$ | $3$ |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $6$ |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $8$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-4962" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({g}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}({g}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n({g}^{-1})^{′}(-2)=\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}({g}^{-1}(-2))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${g}^{-1}(-2)=1$ and ${g}^{′}(1)=8.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({g}^{-1})^{′}(-2) & =\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}({g}^{-1}(-2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-4962" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fc644e02aa9c85304749abccec144ff0b1ac05836a7caf34f8e8f3110207947d"} {"question" "q-4962" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-4962" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{8}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-4962" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{8}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-110272" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The following table gives the values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(4)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $6$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $5$ |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $-3$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-110272" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function ${f}^{-1}(x)g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(x)=\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}+{g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(4)=\\frac{g(4)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))}+{g}^{′}(4){f}^{-1}(4).\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(4)=3,$ $g(4)=-1,$ ${g}^{′}(4)=5,$ and ${f}^{′}(3)=-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(4) & =\\frac{g(4)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))}+{g}^{′}(4){f}^{-1}(4) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{f}^{′}(3)}+5\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{-2}+15 \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-110272" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fc644e02aa9c85304749abccec144ff0b1ac05836a7caf34f8e8f3110207947d"} {"question" "q-110272" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-110272" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "15"] ["math" "\\frac{15}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{28}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{31}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{46}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-110272" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{31}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-342596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find ${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)$ of the product of functions, given the following table of values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $3$ | $-1$ |\n\n${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-342596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $g(x){f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x)+\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(2)={g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2)+\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(2)=1,$ $g(2)=-1,$ ${g}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2},$ and ${f}^{′}(1)=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & ={g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2)+\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1+\\frac{(-1)}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{(-1)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-342596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fc644e02aa9c85304749abccec144ff0b1ac05836a7caf34f8e8f3110207947d"} {"question" "q-342596" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-342596" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-75834" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The following table gives the values of functions $g(x)$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({g}^{-1})^{′}(2).$\n\n| **$x$** | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $3$ | $-6$ |\n| $5$ | $2$ | $-5$ |\n| $7$ | $-2$ | $2$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-75834" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({g}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}({g}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n({g}^{-1})^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}({g}^{-1}(2))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${g}^{-1}(2)=5$ and ${g}^{′}(5)=-5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({g}^{-1})^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}({g}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-5)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-75834" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fc644e02aa9c85304749abccec144ff0b1ac05836a7caf34f8e8f3110207947d"} {"question" "q-75834" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-75834" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{5}"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{6}"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{8}"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{9}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-75834" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-312584" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find ${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)$ of the product of functions, given the following table of values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-4$ | $2$ | $4$ | $-3$ |\n| $5$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\n${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-312584" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $g(x){f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x)+\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(2)={g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2)+\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(2)=5,$ $g(2)=4,$ ${g}^{′}(2)=-3,$ and ${f}^{′}(5)=-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & ={g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2)+\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 5+\\frac{4}{{f}^{′}(5)} \\\\  & =-15+\\frac{4}{(-1)} \\\\  & =-15-4 \\\\  & =-19.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-312584" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fc644e02aa9c85304749abccec144ff0b1ac05836a7caf34f8e8f3110207947d"} {"question" "q-312584" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-312584" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-19"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-312589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2).$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-312589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(0).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(0)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(0) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $x=0$ since the logarithm is not defined here. Therefore, ${f}^{-1}(0)=3.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2) \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x-2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =2\\cdot 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-2)+\\frac{{3}^{2}}{3-2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-312589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fc644e02aa9c85304749abccec144ff0b1ac05836a7caf34f8e8f3110207947d"} {"question" "q-312589" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-312589" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{9}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-342621" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=x-\\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{3}}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain $x\\ge 1,$ determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-342621" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(1).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(1)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(1) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =1 \\\\ x-\\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{3}} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{3}} & =x-1 \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{3} & =(x-1)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{3} & ={x}^{2}-2x+1 \\\\ x+1 & =3({x}^{2}-2x+1) \\\\ x+1 & =3{x}^{2}-6x+3 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-7x+2 & =0 \\\\ (3x-1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3},2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\ge 1,$ we have ${f}^{-1}(1)=2.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =x-\\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{3}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =1-\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{3}}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =1-\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{\\frac{2+1}{3}}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =1-\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{1}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =1-\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{5}{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-342621" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fc644e02aa9c85304749abccec144ff0b1ac05836a7caf34f8e8f3110207947d"} {"question" "q-342621" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-342621" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{6}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-76106" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{2x-1}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-76106" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(3),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(3)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(3).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(3)=x.$ For $x>\\frac{1}{2}$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(3) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =3 \\\\ \\sqrt{2x-1} & =3 \\\\ 2x-1 & =9 \\\\ 2x & =10 \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(5),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{2x-1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(5) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 5-1}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(5) & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(3) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-76106" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fc644e02aa9c85304749abccec144ff0b1ac05836a7caf34f8e8f3110207947d"} {"question" "q-76106" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-76106" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-76106" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-110341" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+5x+2.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-110341" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(2)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ {x}^{3}+5x+2 & =2 \\\\ x({x}^{2}+5) & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(2)=0.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{3}+5x+2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+5 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3\\cdot 0+5 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-110341" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fc644e02aa9c85304749abccec144ff0b1ac05836a7caf34f8e8f3110207947d"} {"question" "q-110341" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-110341" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-5"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{5}"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-110341" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-312588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)={4}^{x}+2.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(6).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(6)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-312588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(6),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(6)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(6))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(6).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(6)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(6) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =6 \\\\ {4}^{x}+2 & =6 \\\\ {4}^{x} & =4 \\\\ {4}^{x} & ={4}^{1} \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(6)=1.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={4}^{x}+2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & ={4}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(6) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(6))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-312588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fc644e02aa9c85304749abccec144ff0b1ac05836a7caf34f8e8f3110207947d"} {"question" "q-312588" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "q-312588" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{4\\ln(4)}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "63047c12093cbc8923a3753ec8330c107cc0f7d0ff702833d1079ea48e6975bf"} {"question" "e-14720" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/assets/8fb287885c948b7e563b5fa878dd204dacbedea311490c4573aa883bff992662.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y=f^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2, 0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$\nThe point $B$ has coordinates $(0,2)$ and lies on $y=f^{-1}(x).$ If $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2},$ what is $(f^{-1})'(0)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/example-14720.json" "file_sha256" "ffc1cea693eadebca1d0722193a9d3e73952f795fc203f350270288f864689b7"} {"question" "e-14720" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $B \\,(0,2)$ lies on $f^{-1},$ we have $f^{-1}(0) = 2.$ We're also told that $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/example-14720.json" "file_sha256" "ffc1cea693eadebca1d0722193a9d3e73952f795fc203f350270288f864689b7"} {"question" "e-14719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{2-3x}{4+5x}$ and the point $P\\left(-1,-5\\right)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ determine the slope of $y=f^{-1}(x)$ at the point $\\left(-5,-1\\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/example-14719.json" "file_sha256" "0cdc319106ac8db98fe7eea74bc885352729f401077493776898eaf46a879804"} {"question" "e-14719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(-1, -5).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2-3x}{4+5x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{22}{(4+5x)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =-22\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(-1, -5)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(-5, -1)$ lies on $y=f^{-1}(x).$ \n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(-5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-22)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{22}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/example-14719.json" "file_sha256" "0cdc319106ac8db98fe7eea74bc885352729f401077493776898eaf46a879804"} {"question" "e-14721" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The following table gives the values of $f(x), f'(x),g(x),$ and $g'(x)$ at some points. Find $(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(1)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ | $g(x)$ | $g'(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $5$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/example-14721.json" "file_sha256" "7498192180fc73b1bdd062e14c0278baa3f43e019b4ac0b34c49325d0038a118"} {"question" "e-14721" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse $f^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(f^{-1})'(x) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $f^{-1}(x)g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(x)=\\dfrac{g(x)}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}+g'(x)f^{-1}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(1) =\\dfrac{g(1)}{f'(f^{-1}(1))}+g'(1)f^{-1}(1).\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain $f^{-1}(1)=2,$ $g(1)=-3,$ $g'(1)=1,$ and $f'(2)=-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(1) & =\\frac{g(1)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))}+{g}^{′}(1){f}^{-1}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{{f}^{′}(2)}+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{-1}+2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/example-14721.json" "file_sha256" "7498192180fc73b1bdd062e14c0278baa3f43e019b4ac0b34c49325d0038a118"} {"question" "e-14722" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x) =3e^{x}-1.$ Given that $y = f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $(f^{-1})'(2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/example-14722.json" "file_sha256" "ed2f86fa3614aef04eaeed13dfadf4132c5147827078633f40125a7cb6af2d3e"} {"question" "e-14722" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine $(f^{-1})'(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(2) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of $f^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation $f^{-1}(2) = x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ 3{e}^{x}-1 & =2 \\\\ 3{e}^{x} & =3 \\\\ {e}^{x} & =1 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f^{-1}(2) = 0.$\n\nNow, we calculate $f'(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3{e}^{x}-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3{e}^{0} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/example-14722.json" "file_sha256" "ed2f86fa3614aef04eaeed13dfadf4132c5147827078633f40125a7cb6af2d3e"}] "usage" {"e-14719" [] "e-14722" [] "e-14720" [] "e-14721" []} "basis" 848 "retractions" [[17592186063913 :question/problem "The figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y=f^{-1}(x)$. The point $A$ has coordinates $(2, 0)$ and lies on $y=f(x)$.\nThe point $B$ has coordinates $(0,2)$ and lies on $y=f^{-1}(x)$. If $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2}$, what is $(f^{-1})'(0)$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/291/Source/Images/d6a83b3149c17f55744d206f77f51649.png)"] [17592186063913 :question/worked-solution "Since $B \\,(0,2)$ lies on $f^{-1}$, we have\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(0) = 2\n$$\n\nWe're also told that\n\n$$\nf'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f^{-1})^{′}(x) &= (1)/(f^{′}(f^{-1}(x)))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f^{-1})^{′}(0) &= (1)/(f^{′}(f^{-1}(0))) \\\\\n&= (1)/(f^{′}(2)) \\\\\n&= (1)/((-\\frac{3}{2})) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186063912 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{2-3x}{4+5x}$ and the point $P\\left(-1,-5\\right)$ that lies on $y=f(x)$. Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P$, determine the slope of $y=f^{-1}(x)$ at the point $\\left(-5,-1\\right)$."] [17592186063912 :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(-1, -5)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{2 - 3x}{4 + 5x} \\\\\nf^{′}(x) &= - (22)/((4 + 5x)^{2}) \\\\\nf^{′}(-1) &= -22\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(-1, -5)$ lies on $y=f(x)$, we know that the corresponding point $(-5, -1)$ lies on\n\n$$\ny=f^{-1}(x)\n$$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f^{-1})^{′}(-5) &= (1)/(f^{′}(f^{-1}(-5))) \\\\\n&= (1)/(f^{′}(-1)) \\\\\n&= (1)/((-22)) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{22}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186063914 :question/problem "The following table gives the values of $f(x), f'(x),g(x)$, and $g'(x)$ at some points. Find $(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(1)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ | $g(x)$ | $g'(x)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $5$ |"] [17592186063914 :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse $f^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(f^{-1})'(x) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $f^{-1}(x)g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(x)=\\dfrac{g(x)}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}+g'(x)f^{-1}(x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1$, we have\n\n$$\n(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(1) =\\dfrac{g(1)}{f'(f^{-1}(1))}+g'(1)f^{-1}(1)\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(1)=2\n$$\n\n$g(1)=-3$, $g'(1)=1$, and $f'(2)=-1$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f^{-1} \\cdot g)^{′}(1) &= (g(1))/(f^{′}(f^{-1}(1))) + g^{′}(1)f^{-1}(1) \\\\\n&= (-3)/(f^{′}(2)) + 1 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= \\frac{-3}{-1} + 2 \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186063915 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) =3e^{x}-1$. Given that $y = f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $(f^{-1})'(2)$."] [17592186063915 :question/worked-solution "To determine $(f^{-1})'(2)$, we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(2) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of $f^{-1}(2)$. To do this, we solve the equation\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(2) = x\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{-1}(2) &= x \\\\\nf(x) &= 2 \\\\\n3e^{x} - 1 &= 2 \\\\\n3e^{x} &= 3 \\\\\ne^{x} &= 1 \\\\\nx &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(2) = 0\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate $f'(0)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= 3e^{x} - 1 \\\\\nf^{′}(x) &= 3e^{x} \\\\\nf^{′}(0) &= 3e^{0} \\\\\nf^{′}(0) &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f^{-1})^{′}(2) &= (1)/(f^{′}(f^{-1}(2))) \\\\\n&= (1)/(f^{′}(0)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
